(A German admiral has remarked that the most valuable naval history lies) Tj T*
(in the despatches and logs of naval officers. Our own Navy Record) Tj T*
(Society by the line it has taken thoroughly endorses this view, and has) Tj T*
(committed itself to the teaching of naval history from the mouths of the) Tj T*
(men who made it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Mr. Taylor's work then must not be taken as a mere record of personal) Tj T*
(adventure, however absorbing it be found from this point of view. As a) Tj T*
(picture of exciting escapes, of coolness and resource at moments of) Tj T*
(acute danger, of well-calculated risks, boldly accepted and obstinately) Tj T*
(carried through, it has few rivals in recent sea-story: but its deeper) Tj T*
(value does not lie here. Over and above its romantic interest it will be) Tj T*
(recognised by students of the naval art as a real and solid contribution) Tj T*
(to history; for it presents to us from the pen of a principal actor the) Tj T*
(most complete account we have of a great blockade in the days of steam.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The important part that blockade plays in naval warfare is a thing) Tj T*
(hardly recognised outside professional ranks. For the general reader,) Tj T*
(the grand man?uvres of a great fleet in chase of the enemy and the) Tj T*
(stirring hours of some decisive action throw into oblivion the tedious) Tj T*
(months of dull, anxious, and exhausting work with which by far the) Tj T*
(greater part of the war is taken up. Yet it is hardly too much to say) Tj T*
(that during the most glorious period of our maritime history nine-tenths) Tj T*
(of the energies of our admirals were devoted to blockade. In the future) Tj T*
(it is possible that it will take even a higher place. Should England) Tj T*
(become engaged with a first-rate foreign power, single-handed, it is a) Tj T*
(recognised fact amongst naval strategists that in a week she could close) Tj T*
(every one of her enemy's ports and have a fleet free to reduce at its) Tj T*
(leisure everything he held beyond the seas. With almost any two Powers) Tj T*
(against her it is probable she could do as much: and it is the) Tj T*
(recognition of this power abroad which gives England, in spite of her) Tj T*
(military weakness, so commanding a position in Europe.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The importance then of studying every scrap of information on the) Tj T*
(subject in order to perfect our knowledge of the art of blockade cannot) Tj T*
(be exaggerated, and Mr. Taylor's simple and straightforward record of) Tj T*
ET
