(his experiences may claim to be perhaps the fullest contribution to the) Tj T*
(subject that as yet exists. Experiences of individual captains we have) Tj T*
(had, and, read with the present work, they are of high value: but Mr.) Tj T*
(Taylor has something more to tell. Not only did he run the blockade) Tj T*
(personally a greater number of times than any one else, but, boy as he) Tj T*
(was at the time, he was the chief organiser of a great and systematised) Tj T*
(attack on the Northern blockade, such as the world had never seen) Tj T*
(before. His operations may be said to have opened a new era in the) Tj T*
(history of blockade, and one which bids fair to have far-reaching) Tj T*
(consequences for every maritime Power.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To make clear his position and its dangers and difficulties a word must) Tj T*
(be said on the general subject of blockade. Blockade, it must be clearly) Tj T*
(borne in mind, is of two kinds, the one military, the other commercial.) Tj T*
(The first concerns the belligerents alone, and consists in one of them,) Tj T*
(who has obtained a working command of the sea, imprisoning the other's) Tj T*
(war fleets in their own ports. It was this form of blockade which) Tj T*
(absorbed by far the greatest part of our naval activity during the great) Tj T*
(French wars. During the American Civil War it was considerably) Tj T*
(practised, and from American sources may be studied in complete detail) Tj T*
(the efforts of the Confederate war-ships to escape the vigilance of) Tj T*
(Federal blockading squadrons. The second form, or commercial blockade,) Tj T*
(is one that principally concerns neutrals, and it was of course to this) Tj T*
(form alone that Mr. Taylor's operations extended.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The International Law which regulates its conditions as between neutrals) Tj T*
(and belligerents is shortly this. A belligerent, if strong enough at sea) Tj T*
(to close one or more ports of his enemy, may give notice to Neutral) Tj T*
(Powers that such port or ports are blockaded, and thereafter if any) Tj T*
(neutral vessel attempts to enter or leave them, the belligerent may) Tj T*
(treat it as an enemy, and may destroy or capture and condemn it as an) Tj T*
(ordinary prize. To run a blockade then is an operation attended with all) Tj T*
(the risks of war. Indeed a blockade-runner is in an even worse position) Tj T*
(than a hostile belligerent; for not being a combatant he may not resist) Tj T*
(the efforts of the blockaders to destroy or capture him. He is entitled) Tj T*
(to escape if he can, but a single shot or blow in his own defence makes) Tj T*
(him a pirate, and a belligerent capturing him may treat him as such. But) Tj T*
(it must always be remembered that for a belligerent to be entitled to) Tj T*
(exercise these high prerogatives he must first have constituted a real) Tj T*
(and effective blockade. A mere declaration that a port is closed is not) Tj T*
(enough. It must be so closely watched and invested with an adequate) Tj T*
(naval force that no neutral can leave or enter without running present) Tj T*
(danger of being sunk or captured.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Analogous to the rights arising out of an effective blockade, and always) Tj T*
(to be clearly distinguished from them, is the right of a belligerent to) Tj T*
(treat as an enemy a neutral vessel carrying contraband of war to his) Tj T*
(enemy's ports, and this right he may always exercise, whether the ports) Tj T*
(in question be effectively blockaded or not.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It was this consideration, no doubt, combined with a desire to preserve) Tj T*
(a strict neutrality and to see the South treated as belligerents and not) Tj T*
