(as mere insurgents, that induced the English Government to recognise the) Tj T*
(Federal blockade as soon as it was declared. At the opening of the war) Tj T*
(the Federal Government, in defiance of International Law, declared the) Tj T*
(whole Southern seaboard under blockade. It was a blockade they were then) Tj T*
(wholly unable to enforce or even to pretend to enforce, but as most of) Tj T*
(our blockade-runners carried contraband of war, there was very little to) Tj T*
(be gained by disputing the Federal pretensions. Some injustice, no) Tj T*
(doubt, was thus done to the South. But it was more than counterbalanced) Tj T*
(by the advantage they gained in that the recognition of the blockade) Tj T*
(made them indisputably belligerents. For these reasons our Government) Tj T*
(thought it wise to waive its neutral rights and submit to a paper) Tj T*
(blockade, which did not exist. As the Northern power increased at sea) Tj T*
(the blockade became more and more effective, and by the time Mr. Taylor) Tj T*
(had got fully to work it may be said to have been something more than a) Tj T*
(pretence. Finally it became very strict and thoroughly effective, and it) Tj T*
(is with this instructive period that his reminiscences are chiefly) Tj T*
(concerned.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This declaration of a blockade that could not be enforced at the time) Tj T*
(was not the only extension of belligerent rights which the Federal) Tj T*
(Government claimed and exercised in respect of blockade. As Mr. Taylor) Tj T*
(fully explains, they did not confine their operations against) Tj T*
(blockade-runners to the established practice of watching the closed) Tj T*
(ports. Not only did they cruise for offenders on the high seas, but they) Tj T*
(intercepted them close to their points of departure, thousands of miles) Tj T*
(from the blockaded ports. Nay, they even went so far as to attempt to) Tj T*
(blockade the neutral ports which the offending vessels were using as) Tj T*
(bases of operations. To most of these claims no objection was made, and) Tj T*
(there is no doubt that in any future war similar operations will be) Tj T*
(recognised without question, as within belligerent rights.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In previous wars a belligerent declaring a blockade had to concern) Tj T*
(himself with little more than turning back ordinary merchantmen who had) Tj T*
(not received notice of the blockade, or cutting off small fry of the) Tj T*
(smuggling type that slipped over from adjacent coasts to take their) Tj T*
(chance of getting in. Such a thing as neutral merchants establishing) Tj T*
(public companies to build fleets of specially designed vessels for the) Tj T*
(avowed purpose of breaking a blockade which was thoroughly effective) Tj T*
(against ordinary types of merchantmen, was a thing unknown to) Tj T*
(International Law. And further, when these merchants stretched their) Tj T*
(rights as neutrals so far as to establish regular bases almost in the) Tj T*
(enemy's waters from which to conduct their revolutionary operations, it) Tj T*
(was obvious that some latitude must be granted to the blockading power.) Tj T*
(No objection, therefore, was ever raised to his cutting off vessels) Tj T*
(avowedly constructed for blockade-running at any point he chose; but) Tj T*
(when he attempted to blockade neutral ports from which they were acting,) Tj T*
(England put her foot down and compelled the Federal cruisers to draw) Tj T*
(off. In this she was clearly within her rights. But although the Federal) Tj T*
(claim to this bold extension of belligerent rights was undoubtedly) Tj T*
(illegal, it was not without provocation. It is another law of blockade) Tj T*
(that a vessel is not "guilty" and cannot be interfered with unless it is) Tj T*
(bound for a blockaded port. The system pursued by Mr. Taylor of) Tj T*
