(establishing depots or bases on British territory close to American) Tj T*
(waters thus greatly increased the difficulties of the cruisers. Goods) Tj T*
(destined for the blockaded ports were consigned first to one of these) Tj T*
(bases, Bermuda, Havana, or the Bahamas, and on their way could not be) Tj T*
(touched by the Northern captains. It was naturally a great temptation to) Tj T*
(these officers as they watched the offensive traffic pouring into the) Tj T*
(runner's bases to see that it did not get out. It is even conceivable) Tj T*
(that England might have been induced to wink at their proceedings. But) Tj T*
(it so happened that the first and only attempt to blockade) Tj T*
(blockade-runners in a British port was made by the very officer who was) Tj T*
(the culprit in the _Trent_ affair, and that too while we were still) Tj T*
(unsoothed from his last violation of our neutrality. The British) Tj T*
(Government, therefore, happened to be in a very irritable mood with the) Tj T*
(North, and though they had hitherto been inexhaustible in their sympathy) Tj T*
