(dilemma. You cannot say, either England has the command or her enemy has) Tj T*
(it. There is still the middle hypothesis, that neither has it. And this) Tj T*
(in all reasonable probability is the worst that could suddenly befall) Tj T*
(us. The destruction of England's command of the sea is no child's play,) Tj T*
(and even if three Powers together succeeded in doing it, it could only) Tj T*
(be at such a sacrifice to themselves as would leave the seas practically) Tj T*
(free to the operations of neutrals. Mr. Taylor's experiences show) Tj T*
(clearly how surprisingly easy it was for bold and expert captains with) Tj T*
(adequate vessels to run the most strict and effective blockades. Were) Tj T*
(England to become engaged in a great war, the first step would be for) Tj T*
(numbers of her mercantile marine to pass to neutral flags, and all these) Tj T*
(vessels with their crews would be ready-made blockade-runners the moment) Tj T*
(there was a call for them. And even assuming that by some extraordinary) Tj T*
(chance the British fleet for a time was suppressed with little or no) Tj T*
(damage to the enemy, the precedents of the American war go to show that) Tj T*
(the navies of three Powers absolutely intact could hardly avail to) Tj T*
(maintain a blockade of such a coast-line as ours.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The conditions of blockade, it is true, have changed, but the balance) Tj T*
(remains much the same. Mr. Taylor considers that search-lights, for) Tj T*
(instance, tell quite as much for one side as the other. Increased speed) Tj T*
(is at least as favourable for running as it is for blockading. Torpedo) Tj T*
(boats seem hardly to affect the balance at all. For while they render) Tj T*
(the position of a blockading squadron less secure than formerly, they on) Tj T*
(the other hand furnish it with ideal patrols. Quick-firing guns are all) Tj T*
(in favour of the blockader, but on the other hand, long-range guns of) Tj T*
(position are all against him, compelling him to keep further to sea and) Tj T*
(so to cover more ground. The extreme importance of invisibility too, on) Tj T*
(which Mr. Taylor insists, shows how great an advantage a runner, able to) Tj T*
(procure good smokeless coal, would have over a force blockading the) Tj T*
(English coast which could not obtain it. On the whole we may safely) Tj T*
(conclude that a commercial blockade is certainly no easier than it was) Tj T*
(in the sixties. Many indications from the following pages show how) Tj T*
(difficult it is to maintain the blockade even of half a dozen ports, if) Tj T*
(you are unable to intercept the regular runners at their points of) Tj T*
(departure. This a force without undisputed mastery of the sea could) Tj T*
(never effect to a sufficient extent. The lesson then that the following) Tj T*
(pages most clearly teaches is, that the danger of the British Isles) Tj T*
(being blockaded by any conceivable combination of hostile Powers, so as) Tj T*
(to reduce her even approximately near starvation, may be dismissed as) Tj T*
(outside the region of practical strategy; and in the next place they) Tj T*
(show us the vast importance of maintaining in our navy an adequate force) Tj T*
(of vessels of a type calculated to render a commercial blockade really) Tj T*
(effective. What Mr. Taylor was able to do with one little steamer to) Tj T*
(prolong Lee's resistance is a lesson to be remembered beside Dundonald's) Tj T*
(operations on the coast of Spain.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Such are a few of the considerations which Mr. Taylor's book suggests.) Tj T*
(Different men will draw different lessons from the facts it presents,) Tj T*
(but its value as the work of a man of unequalled experience in the) Tj T*
(working of a great blockade will be admitted by all: and whatever weight) Tj T*
(may be attached to the author's conclusions from his practical) Tj T*
