(a skinned nose and barked shins. My fellow-passengers, however, were) Tj T*
(evidently suspicious regarding my condition of mind, and looked very) Tj T*
(much askance when I appeared at dinner, thinking no doubt that I was a) Tj T*
(lunatic and my friend my keeper.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If that voyage had been almost enough to extinguish all the ardour I had) Tj T*
(for the life before me, Nassau was enough to set it well aflame again.) Tj T*
(The very thought of the place and of the exciting life there in those) Tj T*
(days, through the brief fever of its prosperity, sets my fancy tingling) Tj T*
(even now.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Those few short years of extravagant importance?so sudden, so fitful, so) Tj T*
(completely passed away?are like a dream, and it seems almost impossible) Tj T*
(to revive a picture of what Nassau was when it found itself the base of) Tj T*
(operations against the great blockade. For centuries the little town had) Tj T*
(slumbered in complete obscurity. Depopulated and abandoned in the old) Tj T*
(days by the Spaniards, it had been occupied in Stuart times by) Tj T*
(Englishmen, and became a haunt of buccaneers. Then followed a century or) Tj T*
(so when it was a counter for diplomatists, and buccaneers settled down) Tj T*
(into wreckers, scraping together hard-earned living from the hurricanes') Tj T*
(leavings, and filling up the dull months between the stormy seasons with) Tj T*
(a little fruit raising and sponge fishing. Thus ingloriously had it) Tj T*
(faded into the obscurest of colonial capitals, with a population of some) Tj T*
(3000 or 4000 souls. There lived and ruled the Governor of the Bahamas,) Tj T*
(and there lived the Chief Justice and the Bishop; these with their) Tj T*
(modest following, and the officers of a West India regiment and a few of) Tj T*
(the leading merchants and their families, made up almost all there was) Tj T*
(of society! Little more eventful ever broke the monotony of their feuds) Tj T*
(and friendships than the visit of one of the ships forming the West) Tj T*
(Indian squadron. Their Lilliputian politics went on from year to year,) Tj T*
(undisturbed and uncared for; there was nothing to mark their place in) Tj T*
(the world but a dusty pigeon-hole somewhere in the Colonial Office,) Tj T*
(which was filled, and emptied, and filled again. Every one was poor and) Tj T*
(every one lazily hopeless of any further development; a few schooners) Tj T*
(that came and went at infrequent intervals sufficed for all the trade) Tj T*
(there was, and the whole air of the sleepy settlement had been one of) Tj T*
(indolent acquiescence in its own obscurity.) Tj T*
