(Then past all expectations came the war, and gold poured into its) Tj T*
(astonished lap. When first I saw the low line of houses nestling in the) Tj T*
(tropical vegetation of their gardens a change had already taken place.) Tj T*
(The blockade had been on foot a bare year, but even then the quiet) Tj T*
(little port had asserted its new importance and was overflowing with the) Tj T*
(turmoil of life. Many influential firms connected with the Southern) Tj T*
(States, and also English ones, had established agencies there, and) Tj T*
(almost every day steamers managed by those agents left the harbour to) Tj T*
(try their luck at evading the blockade or arrived with cargoes of cotton) Tj T*
(from the beleagured ports. Of course, seeing that Nassau was only some) Tj T*
(560 miles from Charleston and 640 from Wilmington, and that, moreover,) Tj T*
(the chain of the Bahama islets extended some hundred miles in the) Tj T*
(direction of those ports, thus providing the extra protection of neutral) Tj T*
(territory for that distance, Nassau was _par excellence_ the base for) Tj T*
(approaching the blockaded Atlantic ports of the South. Bermuda was its) Tj T*
(rival, but only in a lesser degree, as it was further off, and its) Tj T*
(conveniences as regards communication and accommodation were less. It is) Tj T*
(some 690 miles distant from Wilmington, the course being somewhat to the) Tj T*
(northward of west, and in the autumn especially it was seldom possible) Tj T*
(to get over without encountering a gale of wind. The one thing necessary) Tj T*
(for the blockading vessels being speed, their hulls were of the lightest) Tj T*
(description; this, coupled with the fact that they were always loaded) Tj T*
(down deep with coal, made a gale of wind an even worse enemy to) Tj T*
(encounter than a Federal cruiser.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Havana was the best base for the Gulf ports, but as New Orleans was) Tj T*
(captured early on in the war, Galveston and Mobile were the only two) Tj T*
(blockaded ports that could be approached from it; and seeing the) Tj T*
(difficulty there was in procuring cotton at those places and of) Tj T*
(disposing of inward cargoes, the trade done with them was a flea-bite) Tj T*
(compared with that from Charleston and Wilmington. At one time the trade) Tj T*
(of these two ports assumed very large proportions; the number of vessels) Tj T*
(employed in it was astonishing, and no sooner was one sunk, stranded,) Tj T*
(burnt, or captured than two more seemed to take her place.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Of Southern firms Messrs. Fraser, Trenholm, and Co. did the largest) Tj T*
(business, as they were not only engaged largely on their own account in) Tj T*
(blockade-running enterprises, but they were also agents for the Southern) Tj T*
(States Government. Their representative in Nassau, Mr. J. B. Lafitte, a) Tj T*
(charming man in every respect, occupied a most prominent position,?in) Tj T*
(fact more prominent than that of the Governor himself, and certainly he) Tj T*
(was remunerated better.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(After Fraser, Trenholm, and Co. came the English firm of Alex. Collie) Tj T*
(and Co., at that time one of great repute, represented by my friend L.) Tj T*
(G. Watson, and they from time to time were possessed of a large fleet of) Tj T*
(runners commanded mostly by naval officers. After them came the house I) Tj T*
(represented, which from first to last owned some fifteen steamers; and) Tj T*
(after them a number of small firms, owning perhaps one, possibly two,) Tj T*
(boats apiece, so that in the aggregate the number of boats and the) Tj T*
(capital employed was enormous.) Tj T*
