(After my disappointment it will easily be imagined how anxious I was to) Tj T*
(know how my new ship was progressing. On reaching Liverpool my first) Tj T*
(care was to visit the yard where she was being built. To my great) Tj T*
(delight I found her almost completed, and a marvel of shipbuilding as it) Tj T*
(seemed to us then. For the _Banshee_, as she was called, may claim to be) Tj T*
(a landmark not only in the development of blockade but also of marine) Tj T*
(architecture. With the exception of a boat built for Livingstone of) Tj T*
(African fame, she was, I believe, the first steel ship ever laid down.) Tj T*
(The new blockade-runner was a paddle boat, built of steel, on) Tj T*
(extraordinarily fine lines, 214 feet long and 20 feet beam, and drew) Tj T*
(only 8 feet of water. Her masts were mere poles without yards, and with) Tj T*
(the least possible rigging. In order to attain greater speed in a) Tj T*
(sea-way she was built with a turtle-back deck forward. She was of 217) Tj T*
(tons net register, and had an anticipated sea speed of eleven knots,) Tj T*
(with a coal consumption of thirty tons a day. Her crew, which included) Tj T*
(three engineers and twelve firemen, consisted of thirty-six hands all) Tj T*
(told.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Steel ship-building was then in its infancy, and the _Banshee_ was the) Tj T*
(first of a fleet that was soon to become famous. There were several) Tj T*
(similar steamers already in hand, and although no one could tell how) Tj T*
(they would behave when exposed to the great seas of the Atlantic, the) Tj T*
(best results were anticipated from the strength and lightness of their) Tj T*
(materials. They were expected to develop a buoyancy beyond everything) Tj T*
(that had yet been seen, and American naval officers awaited their) Tj T*
(arrival on the scene of activity with an interest as great as ours.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The _Banshee_ was ready for sea early in 1863, and I had the) Tj T*
(satisfaction of finding myself steaming down the Mersey in the _first_) Tj T*
(steel vessel that ever crossed the Atlantic.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Like most first attempts, however, she was far from a success, and by) Tj T*
(the time we reached Queenstown she had betrayed serious defects. To) Tj T*
(begin with, the speed she developed was extremely disappointing. With) Tj T*
(the idea of protecting her boilers from shot, they had been constructed) Tj T*
(so low that they had not sufficient steam space, and, worse than this,) Tj T*
(the plates of which she was built, being only an 1/8 and 3/16 of an inch) Tj T*
(thick, she proved so weak that her decks leaked like a sieve. It was) Tj T*
(found absolutely necessary to put into Queenstown and make such) Tj T*
(alterations as were possible. Thus three more weeks were lost, and when) Tj T*
(at last we were able to put out again it was only to be driven back off) Tj T*
(the Fastnet by a south-westerly gale, which swept the _Banshee_ clean) Tj T*
(from stem to stern of everything on deck, filled her fore stoke-hole,) Tj T*
