(      notwithstanding her heavy requisitions on it for the post) Tj T*
(      hospital, the sick and wounded soldiers and sailors always) Tj T*
(      being a subject of her tenderest solicitude, and often the) Tj T*
(      hard worked and poorly fed coloured hands blessed the) Tj T*
(      little lady of the cottage for a tempting treat.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(      Full of stirring events were the two years passed in the) Tj T*
(      cottage on Confederate Point. The drowning of Mrs. Rose) Tj T*
(      Greenough, the famous Confederate spy, off Fort Fisher,) Tj T*
(      and the finding of her body, which was tenderly cared for,) Tj T*
(      and the rescue from the waves, half dead, of Professor) Tj T*
(      Holcombe, and his restoration, were incidents never to be) Tj T*
(      forgotten. Her fox-hunting with horse and hounds, the narrow) Tj T*
(      escapes of friendly vessels, the fights over blockade-runners) Tj T*
(      driven ashore, the execution of deserters, and the) Tj T*
(      loss of an infant son, whose little spirit went out with the) Tj T*
(      tide one sad summer night, all contributed to the reality of) Tj T*
(      this romantic life.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(      When Porter's fleet appeared off Fort Fisher, December) Tj T*
(      1864, it was storm-bound for several days, and the little) Tj T*
(      family with their household goods were sent across the) Tj T*
(      river to "Orton," before Butler's powder-ship blew up.) Tj T*
(      After the Christmas victory over Porter and Butler, the) Tj T*
(      little heroine insisted upon coming back to her cottage,) Tj T*
(      although her husband had procured a home of refuge in) Tj T*
(      Cumberland county. General Whiting protested against) Tj T*
(      her running the risk, for on dark nights her husband could) Tj T*
(      not leave the fort, but she said, "if the firing became too) Tj T*
(      hot she would run behind the sand hills as she had done) Tj T*
(      before," and come she would.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(      The fleet reappeared unexpectedly on the night of the) Tj T*
(      12th of January 1865. It was a dark night, and when) Tj T*
(      the lights of the fleet were reported her husband sent a) Tj T*
(      courier to the cottage to instruct her to pack up quickly) Tj T*
(      and be prepared to leave with children and nurse as soon) Tj T*
(      as he could come to bid them good-bye. The garrison) Tj T*
(      barge, with a trusted crew, was stationed at Craig's Landing,) Tj T*
ET
