(This placed us in a pretty predicament, as it showed our whereabouts to) Tj T*
(two cruisers which were following us, one on each quarter. They at once) Tj T*
(opened a furious cannonade upon us; however, although shells were) Tj T*
(bursting all around and shot flying over us, all hands worked with a) Tj T*
(will, and we soon extinguished the flames, which were acting as a) Tj T*
(treacherous beacon to our foes. Fortunately the night was intensely) Tj T*
(dark, and nothing could be seen beyond a radius of thirty or forty) Tj T*
(yards, so, thanks to this, we were soon enabled, by altering our helm,) Tj T*
(to give our pursuers the slip, whilst they probably kept on their) Tj T*
(course.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We had still the other enemy to deal with; but our chief engineer and) Tj T*
(his staff had meanwhile been hard at work and had turned on the) Tj T*
("bilge-injection" and "donkey-pumps." Still, the leak was gaining upon) Tj T*
(us, and it became evident that the severe shaking which the ship got) Tj T*
(when run aground had started the plates in her bottom. The mud had been) Tj T*
(sucked up when she lay in the river at Wilmington, thus temporarily) Tj T*
(repairing the damage; but when she got into the sea-way the action of) Tj T*
(the water opened them again. Even the steam pumps now could not prevent) Tj T*
(the water from gradually increasing; four of our eight furnaces were) Tj T*
(extinguished, and the firemen were working up to their middles in water.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It was a critical time when daylight broke, dull and threatening. The) Tj T*
(captain was at the wheel, and I at the mast-head \(all other hands being) Tj T*
(employed at the pumps, and even baling\), when, not four miles off, I) Tj T*
(sighted a cruiser broadside on. She turned round as if preparing to give) Tj T*
(chase, and I thought we were done for, as we could not have got more) Tj T*
(than three or four knots an hour out of our crippled boat. To my great) Tj T*
(joy, however, I found our alarm was needless, for she evidently had not) Tj T*
(seen us, and instead of heading turned her stern towards us and) Tj T*
(disappeared into a thick bank of clouds.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Still we were far from being out of danger, as the weather became worse) Tj T*
(and worse and the wind increased in force until it was blowing almost a) Tj T*
(gale. Things began to look as ugly as they could, and even Capper lost) Tj T*
(hope: I shall never forget the expression on his face as he came up to) Tj T*
(me and said, in his gruff voice, "I say, Mr. Taylor! the beggar's going,) Tj T*
(the beggar's going," pointing vehemently downwards. "What the devil do) Tj T*
(you mean!" I exclaimed. "Why, we are going to lose the ship and our) Tj T*
(lives too," was the answer. It is not possible for any one unacquainted) Tj T*
(with Capper to appreciate this scene. Sturdy, thickset, nearly as broad) Tj T*
(as he was long, and with the gruffest manner but kindest heart,?although) Tj T*
(a rough diamond and absolutely without fear. With the exception of) Tj T*
(Steele he was the best blockade-running captain we had.) Tj T*
