(In order to save the steamer and our lives we decided that desperate) Tj T*
(remedies must be resorted to, so again the unlucky deck cargo had to be) Tj T*
(sacrificed. The good effect of this was soon visible; we began to gain) Tj T*
(on the water, and were able, by degrees, to relight our extinguished) Tj T*
(fires. But the struggle continued to be a most severe one, for just when) Tj T*
(we began to obtain a mastery over the water the donkey-engine broke) Tj T*
(down, and before we could repair it the water increased sensibly, nearly) Tj T*
(putting out our fires again. So the struggle went on for sixty hours,) Tj T*
(when we were truly thankful to steam into Nassau harbour and beach the) Tj T*
(ship. It was a very narrow escape, for within twenty minutes after) Tj T*
(stopping her engines the vessel had sunk to the level of the water.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I had the _Will-o'-the-Wisp_ raised, hauled up on the slip, and repaired) Tj T*
(at an enormous expense before she was fit again for sea. Subsequently) Tj T*
(she made several trips, but as I found her a constant source of delay) Tj T*
(and expenditure I decided to sell her. After having her cobbled up with) Tj T*
(plenty of putty and paint, I was fortunate enough to open negotiations) Tj T*
(with some Jews with a view to her purchase. Having settled all) Tj T*
(preliminaries we arranged for a trial trip, and after a very sumptuous) Tj T*
(lunch I proceeded to run her over a measured mile for the benefit of the) Tj T*
(would-be purchasers. I need scarcely mention that we subjected her) Tj T*
(machinery to the utmost strain, bottling up steam to a pressure of which) Tj T*
(our present Board of Trade, with its motherly care for our lives, would) Tj T*
(express strong disapproval. The log line was whisked merrily over the) Tj T*
(stern of the _Will-o'-the-Wisp_, with the satisfactory result that she) Tj T*
(logged 17-1/2 knots. The Jews were delighted, so was I; and the bargain) Tj T*
(was clinched. I fear, however, that their joy was short-lived; a few) Tj T*
(weeks afterwards when attempting to steam into Galveston she was run) Tj T*
(ashore and destroyed by the Federals. When we ran into that port a few) Tj T*
(months afterwards in the second _Banshee_ we saw her old bones on the) Tj T*
(beach.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(After this I made a trip in a new boat that had just been sent out to) Tj T*
(me, the _Wild Dayrell_. And a beauty she was, very strong, a perfect) Tj T*
(sea-boat, and remarkably well engined.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Our voyage in was somewhat exciting, as about three o'clock in the) Tj T*
(afternoon, while making for the Fort Caswell entrance \(not Fort Fisher\),) Tj T*
(we were sighted by a Federal cruiser, who immediately gave chase. We) Tj T*
(soon found however, that we had the heels of our friend, but it left us) Tj T*
(the alternative of going out to sea or being chased straight into the) Tj T*
(jaws of the blockaders off the bar before darkness came on. Under these) Tj T*
(circumstances what course to take was a delicate point to decide, but we) Tj T*
(solved the problem by slowing down just sufficiently to keep a few miles) Tj T*
(ahead of our chaser, hoping that darkness would come on before we made) Tj T*
(the fleet or they discovered us. Just as twilight was drawing in we made) Tj T*
(them out; cautiously we crept on, feeling certain that our friend astern) Tj T*
(was rapidly closing up on us. Every moment we expected to hear shot) Tj T*
(whistling around us. So plainly could we see the sleepy blockaders that) Tj T*
(it seemed almost impossible we should escape their notice. Whether they) Tj T*
(did not expect a runner to make an attempt so early in the evening, or) Tj T*
