(whether it was sheer good luck on our part, I know not, but we ran) Tj T*
(through the lot without being seen or without having a shot fired at us.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Our anxieties, however, were not yet over, as our pilot \(a new hand\)) Tj T*
(lost his reckoning and put us ashore on the bar. Fortunately the flood) Tj T*
(tide was rising fast, and we refloated, bumping over stern first in a) Tj T*
(most inglorious fashion, and anchored off Fort Caswell before 7 P.M.?a) Tj T*
(record performance. Soon after anchoring and while enjoying the usual) Tj T*
(cocktail we saw a great commotion among the blockaders, who were) Tj T*
(throwing up rockets and flashing lights, evidently in answer to signals) Tj T*
(from the cruiser which had so nearly chased us into their midst.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(When we came out we met with equally good luck, as the night was pitch) Tj T*
(dark and the weather very squally. No sooner did we clear the bar than) Tj T*
(we put our helm aport, ran down the coast, and then stood boldly) Tj T*
(straight out to sea without interference: and it was perhaps as well we) Tj T*
(had such good fortune, as before this I had discovered that our pilot) Tj T*
(was of a very indifferent calibre, and that courage was not our) Tj T*
(captain's most prominent characteristic. The poor _Wild Dayrell_) Tj T*
(deserved a better commander, and consequently a better fate than befell) Tj T*
(her. She was lost on her second trip, entirely through the want of pluck) Tj T*
(on the part of her captain, who ran her ashore some miles to the north) Tj T*
(of Fort Fisher; as _he_ said in order to avoid capture,?to my mind a) Tj T*
(fatal excuse for any blockade-running captain to make. 'Twere far better) Tj T*
(to be sunk by shot and escape in the boats if possible. I am quite) Tj T*
(certain that if Steele had commanded her on that trip she would never) Tj T*
(have been put ashore, and the chances are that she would have come) Tj T*
(through all right.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I never forgave myself for not unshipping the captain on my return to) Tj T*
(Nassau; my only excuse was that there was no good man available to) Tj T*
(replace him with, and he was a particular protégé of my chiefs. But such) Tj T*
(considerations should not have weighed, and if I had had the courage of) Tj T*
(my convictions it is probable the _Wild Dayrell_ would have proved as) Tj T*
(successful as any of our steamers.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(About this time I had two other new boats sent out, the _Stormy Petrel_) Tj T*
(and the _Wild Rover_, both good boats, very fast, and distinct) Tj T*
(improvements on the _Banshee_ No. 1 and _Will-o'-the-Wisp_. The _Stormy) Tj T*
(Petrel_ had, however, very bad luck, as after getting safely in and) Tj T*
(anchoring behind Fort Fisher she settled as the tide went down on a) Tj T*
(submerged anchor, the fluke of which went through her bottom, and) Tj T*
(despite all efforts she became a total wreck: this was one of the most) Tj T*
(serious and unlucky losses I had. The _Wild Rover_ was more successful,) Tj T*
(as she made five round trips, on one of which I went in her. She) Tj T*
(survived the war, and I eventually sent her to South America, where she) Tj T*
(was sold for a good sum.) Tj T*
