(We had in the early part of the war a depôt at Bermuda as well as at) Tj T*
(Nassau, and Frank Hurst was at that time my brother agent there. I went) Tj T*
(there twice, once in the first _Banshee_, and once from Halifax, after a) Tj T*
(trip to Canada in order to recruit from a bad attack of yellow fever;) Tj T*
(but I never liked Bermuda, and later on we transferred Hurst and his) Tj T*
(agency to Nassau, which was more convenient in many ways and nearer) Tj T*
(Wilmington. Moreover I had to face the contingency, which afterwards) Tj T*
(occurred, of the Atlantic ports being closed and our being driven to the) Tj T*
(Gulf. The Mudians, however, were a kind, hospitable lot, and made a) Tj T*
(great deal of us, and there was a much larger naval and military society) Tj T*
(stationed there than in Nassau. They had suffered from a severe outbreak) Tj T*
(of yellow fever, and the 3rd Buffs, who were in garrison at the time,) Tj T*
(had been almost decimated by it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It was on my second trip to the island that one of the finest boats we) Tj T*
(ever possessed, called the _Night Hawk_, came out, and I concluded to) Tj T*
(run in with her. She was a new side-wheel steamer of some 600 tons) Tj T*
(gross, rigged as a fore and aft schooner, with two funnels, 220 feet) Tj T*
(long, 21-1/2 feet beam, and 11 feet in depth; a capital boat for the) Tj T*
(work, fast, strong, of light draught, and a splendid sea-boat?a great) Tj T*
(merit in a blockade-runner that sometimes has to be forced in all) Tj T*
(weathers. The _Night Hawk's_ career was a very eventful one, and she) Tj T*
(passed an unusually lively night off Fort Fisher on her first attempt.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Soon after getting under weigh our troubles began. We ran ashore outside) Tj T*
(Hamilton, one of the harbours of Bermuda, and hung on a coral reef for a) Tj T*
(couple of hours. There loomed before us the dismal prospect of delay for) Tj T*
(repairs, or, still worse, the chance of springing a leak and) Tj T*
(experiencing such difficulties and dangers as we had undergone on the) Tj T*
(_Will-o'-the-Wisp_, but fortunately we came off without damage and were) Tj T*
(able to proceed on our voyage.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Another anxiety now engrossed my mind: the captain was an entirely new) Tj T*
(hand, and nearly all the crew were green at the work; moreover, the) Tj T*
(Wilmington pilot was quite unknown to me, and I could see from the) Tj T*
(outset that he was very nervous and badly wanting in confidence. What) Tj T*
(would I not have given for our trusty Tom Burroughs. However, we had to) Tj T*
(make the best of it, as, owing to the demand, the supply of competent) Tj T*
(pilots was not nearly sufficient, and towards the close of the blockade) Tj T*
(the so-called pilots were no more than boatmen or men who had been) Tj T*
(trading in and out of Wilmington or Charleston in coasters.) Tj T*
