(Notwithstanding my fears, all went well on the way across, and the) Tj T*
(_Night_ _Hawk_ proved to be everything that could be desired in speed) Tj T*
(and seaworthiness.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We had sighted unusually few craft, and nothing eventful occurred until) Tj T*
(the third night. Soon after midnight we found ourselves uncomfortably) Tj T*
(near a large vessel. It was evident that we had been seen, as we heard) Tj T*
(them beating to quarters and were hailed. We promptly sheered off and) Tj T*
(went full speed ahead, greeted by a broadside which went across our) Tj T*
(stern.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(When we arrived within striking distance of Wilmington bar the pilot was) Tj T*
(anxious to go in by Smith's inlet, but as he acknowledged that he knew) Tj T*
(very little about it I concluded it was better to keep to the new inlet) Tj T*
(passage, where, at all events, we should have the advantage of our good) Tj T*
(friend Lamb to protect us; and I felt that as I myself knew the place so) Tj T*
(well, this was the safest course to pursue. We were comparatively well) Tj T*
(through the fleet, although heavily fired at, and arrived near to the) Tj T*
(bar, passing close by two Northern launches which were lying almost upon) Tj T*
(it. Unfortunately it was dead low water, and although I pressed the) Tj T*
(pilot to give our boat a turn round, keeping under weigh, and to wait a) Tj T*
(while until the tide made, he was so demoralised by the firing we had) Tj T*
(gone through and the nearness of the launches, which were constantly) Tj T*
(throwing up rockets, that he insisted upon putting her at the bar, and,) Tj T*
(as I feared, we grounded on it forward, and with the strong flood-tide) Tj T*
(quickly broached-to, broadside on to the Northern breaker. We kept our) Tj T*
(engines going for some time?but to no purpose, as we found we were only) Tj T*
(being forced by the tide more on to the breakers. Therefore we stopped,) Tj T*
(and all at once found our friends, the two launches, close aboard: they) Tj T*
(had discovered we were ashore, and had made up their minds to attack us.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(At once all was in confusion; the pilot and signalman rushed to the) Tj T*
(dinghy, lowered it, and made good their escape; the captain lost his) Tj T*
(head and disappeared; and the crews of the launches, after firing) Tj T*
(several volleys, one of which slightly wounded me, rowed in to board us) Tj T*
(on each sponson. Just at this moment I suddenly recollected that our) Tj T*
(private despatches, which ought to have been thrown overboard, were) Tj T*
(still in the starboard life-boat. I rushed to it, but found the lanyard) Tj T*
(to which the sinking weight was attached was foul of one of the thwarts;) Tj T*
(I tugged and tugged, but to no purpose, so I sung out for a knife which) Tj T*
(was handed to me by a fireman, and I cut the line and pitched the bag) Tj T*
(overboard as the Northerners jumped on board. Eighteen months afterwards) Tj T*
(that fireman accosted me in the Liverpool streets, saying, "Mr. Taylor,) Tj T*
(do you remember my lending you a knife." "Of course I do," I replied,) Tj T*
(giving him a tip at which he was mightily pleased: poor fellow, he had) Tj T*
(been thirteen months in a Northern prison.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(When the Northerners jumped on board they were terribly excited. I don't) Tj T*
(know whether they expected resistance or not, but they acted more like) Tj T*
(maniacs than sane men, firing their revolvers and cutting right and left) Tj T*
(with their cutlasses. I stood in front of the men on the poop and said) Tj T*
(that we surrendered, but all the reply I received from the lieutenant) Tj T*
