(commanding was, "Oh, you surrender, do you?..." accompanied by a string) Tj T*
(of the choicest Yankee oaths and sundry reflections upon my parentage;) Tj T*
(whereupon he fired his revolver twice point blank at me not two yards) Tj T*
(distant: it was a miracle he did not kill me, as I heard the bullets) Tj T*
(whiz past my head. This roused my wrath, and I expostulated in the) Tj T*
(strongest terms upon his firing on unarmed men; he then cooled down,) Tj T*
(giving me into the charge of two of his men, one of whom speedily) Tj T*
(possessed himself of my binoculars. Fortunately, as I had no guard to my) Tj T*
(watch, they didn't discover it, and I have it still.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Finding they could not get the ship off, and afraid, I presume, of Lamb) Tj T*
(and his men coming to our rescue, the Federals commenced putting the) Tj T*
(captain \(who had been discovered behind a boat!\) and the crew into the) Tj T*
(boats; they then set the ship on fire fore and aft, and she soon began) Tj T*
(to blaze merrily. At this moment one of our firemen, an Irishman, sung) Tj T*
(out, "Begorra, we shall all be in the air in a minute, the ship is full) Tj T*
(of gunpowder!" No sooner did the Northern sailors hear this than a panic) Tj T*
(seized them, and they rushed to their boats, threatening to leave their) Tj T*
(officers behind if they did not come along. The men who were holding me) Tj T*
(dropped me like a hot potato, and to my great delight jumped into their) Tj T*
(boat, and away they rowed as fast as they could, taking all our crew,) Tj T*
(with the exception of the second officer, one of the engineers, four) Tj T*
(seamen and myself, as prisoners.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We chuckled at our lucky escape, but we were not out of the wood yet, as) Tj T*
(we had only a boat half stove in, in which to reach the shore through) Tj T*
(some 300 yards of surf, and we were afraid at any moment that our) Tj T*
(enemies finding there was no powder on board might return. We made a) Tj T*
(feeble effort to put the fire out, but it had gained too much headway,) Tj T*
(and although I offered the men with me £50 apiece to stand by me and) Tj T*
(persevere, they were too demoralised and began to lower the shattered) Tj T*
(boat, swearing that they would leave me behind if I didn't come with) Tj T*
(them. There was nothing for it but to go, yet the passage through the) Tj T*
(boiling surf seemed more dangerous to my mind than remaining on the) Tj T*
(burning ship. The blockaders immediately opened fire when they knew) Tj T*
(their own men had left the _Night Hawk_, and that she was burning; and) Tj T*
(Lamb's great shells hurtling over our heads, and those from the) Tj T*
(blockading fleet bursting all around us, formed a weird picture. In) Tj T*
(spite of the hail of shot and shell and the dangers of the boiling surf,) Tj T*
(we reached the shore in safety, wet through, and glad I was in my state) Tj T*
(of exhaustion from loss of blood and fatigue to be welcomed by Lamb's) Tj T*
(orderly officer.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The poor _Night Hawk_ was now a sheet of flame, and I thought it was all) Tj T*
(up with her; and indeed it would have been had it not been for Lamb,) Tj T*
(who, calling for volunteers from his garrison, sent off two or three) Tj T*
(boat loads of men to her, and when I came down to the beach, after) Tj T*
(having my wound dressed and a short rest, I was delighted to find the) Tj T*
(fire had sensibly decreased. I went on board, and after some hours of) Tj T*
(hard work the fire was extinguished. But what a wreck she was!) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Luckily with the rising tide she had bumped over the bank, and was now) Tj T*
