(The dangers and discomforts at sea were not the only excitements which a) Tj T*
(blockade-runner experienced. As the blockade-running fleet of which I) Tj T*
(had charge extended, not only was an increase in my office staff in) Tj T*
(Nassau entailed, but a good deal of travelling by rail to and fro) Tj T*
(between Wilmington and Richmond, for the purpose of negotiations with) Tj T*
(the heads of departments there regarding the contracts we had with them,) Tj T*
(and upon various other matters.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(These trips involved an enormous amount of fatigue, worry, excitement,) Tj T*
(and even danger, as it was no easy matter latterly to get in and out of) Tj T*
(the beleaguered city safely; the railway journey itself, which often) Tj T*
(extended over a couple of days and nights, was an affair of great) Tj T*
(discomfort, the permanent way being anything but permanent, and the) Tj T*
(rolling stock too often rolling elsewhere than upon the rails. It was) Tj T*
(considered a joke in those days to assert that a journey from Wilmington) Tj T*
(to Richmond was almost as dangerous as an engagement with the enemy. The) Tj T*
(only place on the train where any approach to comfort was obtainable was) Tj T*
(in the conductor's car, the entrée to which I generally contrived to) Tj T*
(secure, aided by a little judicious palm-greasing and the possession of) Tj T*
(a brandy bottle or two; but the latter had its disadvantages, as the) Tj T*
(word was soon passed round that there was a Britisher on board the train) Tj T*
(with some _real_ good brandy. And it was considered the duty of every) Tj T*
(one to whom I had stood a drink to introduce a friend who wanted one) Tj T*
(badly; consequently the brandy was generally used up on the outward) Tj T*
(trip, and there was little left for the return. But it was great) Tj T*
(pleasure to be able to quench the poor fellows' thirst, more especially) Tj T*
(the wounded, with whom the cars were often filled to overflowing.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
