(As a rule my good friend Heiliger, Confederate Agent at Nassau, used to) Tj T*
(entrust me with despatches, the carriage of which provided me with a) Tj T*
(pass which much facilitated my journeys; but on one occasion towards the) Tj T*
(end of the war the possession of these despatches made it a little) Tj T*
(awkward for me. I had arrived one afternoon at Petersburg, which is) Tj T*
(about fifteen miles from Richmond, and found a tremendous hubbub going) Tj T*
(on. Butler, having attacked the place with his corps, hoped to take it) Tj T*
(and then turn the Confederate flank. Although it was but poorly) Tj T*
(defended, being held by some 1500 recruits and boys, they kept their) Tj T*
(ground, entrenched about a mile outside the town.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It was while this first attack was in progress that I arrived on the) Tj T*
(scene, and recognising the gravity of the position, if the place were) Tj T*
(taken and despatches found upon me \(an Englishman\), I went to the) Tj T*
(Commissary-General and asked him to provide me with a horse to take me) Tj T*
(to Richmond. He said this was impossible, but that they had telegraphed) Tj T*
(for reinforcements, and that Hoke's division was expected by train in an) Tj T*
