(of getting the empty return train. It was a weary and anxious wait, as) Tj T*
(we could hear the attack going on and feared the defence would every) Tj T*
(moment be overpowered. However, a short time before daylight we heard) Tj T*
(the train approaching, and soon afterwards it steamed in, crowded even) Tj T*
(on the roofs of carriages by Hoke's men, who were promptly detrained and) Tj T*
(hurried off at the double to the scene of action?a welcome) Tj T*
(reinforcement. I got in the train, and we started for Richmond. We had) Tj T*
(only proceeded a few miles when, in the gray dawn, we saw a body of) Tj T*
(Butler's cavalry galloping as hard as they could to intercept us and) Tj T*
(tear up the line in front. Our engineer, however, equal to the occasion,) Tj T*
(put on full steam, and we just managed to get ahead of them. Seeing they) Tj T*
(were too late, they drew up alongside the track and potted at us with) Tj T*
(their carbines, without, however, wounding any one. They then at once) Tj T*
(tore up the rails in our rear.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Being under fire in a train was a curious experience, and perhaps more) Tj T*
(exciting for me than the others, as I had my hand on the blessed) Tj T*
(despatches, uncertain what to do. Fortunately we arrived safely at) Tj T*
(Richmond, and I was very glad to be rid of my responsibilities. This was) Tj T*
(the last train that got in on the direct Wilmington line; after that, in) Tj T*
(order to get in and out, we had to make a long detour viâ Danville.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I found Richmond in a great state of excitement; the Northern attack had) Tj T*
(become more animated; the investment was more stringent; the booming of) Tj T*
(heavy guns was heard night and day; and hourly reports were brought from) Tj T*
(the front. It was upon this visit that I accompanied Lee's Headquarter) Tj T*
(staff on the celebrated march along the south side of the James river,) Tj T*
(when he marched rapidly to Petersburg in order to confront the) Tj T*
(Northerners' sudden change of front on that town. Upon a previous) Tj T*
(occasion I had made the acquaintance of the great General, and on this) Tj T*
(one I breakfasted with him. Shortly afterwards the march, which was very) Tj T*
(exciting, began. We were constantly in close touch with the enemy,?at) Tj T*
(one time marching through the woods, which were being shelled by the) Tj T*
(Northern gunboats in the James river?at another time skirmishing at) Tj T*
