() Tj T*
(The following is an extract from a letter dated 15th January 1865,) Tj T*
(written to my chiefs after this visit to Richmond.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(      Altogether I think the Confederate Government is going) Tj T*
(      to the _bad_, and if they don't take care the Confederacy) Tj T*
(      will go too. I never saw things look so gloomy, and I think) Tj T*
(      spring will finish them unless they make a change for) Tj T*
(      the better. Georgia is gone, and they say Sherman is) Tj T*
(      going to seize Branchville; if he does, Charleston and) Tj T*
(      Wilmington will be done?and if Wilmington goes Lee) Tj T*
(      has to evacuate Richmond and retire into Tennessee. He) Tj T*
(      told me the other day, that if they did not keep Wilmington) Tj T*
(      he could not save Richmond. They nearly had Fort Fisher?they) Tj T*
(      were within sixty yards of it?and had they pushed) Tj T*
(      on as they ought to have done could have taken it. It) Tj T*
(      was a terrific bombardment; they estimate that about) Tj T*
(      40,000 shells were sent into it. Colonel Lamb behaved) Tj T*
(      like a brick?splendidly. I got the last of the Whitworths) Tj T*
(      in, and they are now at the Fort. They are very hard up) Tj T*
(      for food in the field, but the _Banshee_ has this time 600) Tj T*
(      barrels of pork and 1500 boxes of meat?enough to feed) Tj T*
(      Lee's army for a month.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The above extract is interesting, as it showed that my diagnosis of the) Tj T*
(position of affairs, written in January 1865, proved correct as to what) Tj T*
(actually happened two or three months later. Sherman _did_ capture) Tj T*
(Branchville, and in consequence Charleston and Wilmington. When the) Tj T*
(latter port fell Lee _was_ forced to evacuate Richmond and retire) Tj T*
(towards Tennessee and eventually capitulate. Had Charleston and) Tj T*
(Wilmington been retained and blockade-running encouraged, instead of) Tj T*
(having obstacles thrown in the way, I am convinced that the condition of) Tj T*
(affairs would have been altered very materially, and perhaps would have) Tj T*
(led to the South obtaining what it had shed so much blood to gain, viz.) Tj T*
(its independence. No doubt at that critical time the North was making) Tj T*
(its last supreme effort, and, had it failed, negotiations would probably) Tj T*
(have been opened up with a view to peace.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The privations of the regular residents in Richmond in those days were) Tj T*
(very great, as food of all kinds was very expensive; but all bore their) Tj T*
(troubles without a murmur, and I think there was more enthusiasm) Tj T*
(displayed there than in any other city in the South; probably because) Tj T*
(the people, with the enemy at their gates, were always in close touch) Tj T*
ET
