(Havana was a great blockade-running centre to and from the Gulf ports,) Tj T*
(but until Wilmington was closed I did not attempt to utilise it, for) Tj T*
(many reasons preferring Nassau and the last named port. I went over) Tj T*
(there, however, several times, partly on business, and partly on) Tj T*
(pleasure, and a lovely city it was. Cuba was then in the heyday of) Tj T*
(success, and no one who had not visited its capital could have imagined) Tj T*
(that such a gay and beautiful city existed in the West Indies. Money) Tj T*
(seemed no object. And fortunately there was plenty, for everything was) Tj T*
(extravagantly dear, and I should think that at that time it was one of) Tj T*
(the most if not the most expensive city in the world to live in.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To us blockade-runners, accustomed to the hard life in the South and the) Tj T*
(contracted surroundings of Nassau, Havana appeared like Paradise; good) Tj T*
(hotels and casinos, a capital theatre, magnificent equipages, military) Tj T*
(bands, handsome women, and, last but not least, the lavish and genial) Tj T*
(hospitality dispensed by our Consul-General, Mr. Crawford, and his) Tj T*
(charming daughters at their house, "Buenos Ayres," made a residence in) Tj T*
(Havana like a rest in an oasis to the weary traveller of the desert. But) Tj T*
(it was not all pleasure, as far as I was concerned. I had my business) Tj T*
(with its anxieties to attend to, and on one of my visits I had a rather) Tj T*
(adventurous trip to Nassau in a small schooner which I had chartered to) Tj T*
