(When all was ready we experienced the greatest difficulty in finding a) Tj T*
(Galveston pilot. Though, owing to the high rate of pay, numbers of men) Tj T*
(were to be found ready to offer their services, it was extremely hard to) Tj T*
(obtain competent men. After considerable delay we had to content) Tj T*
(ourselves at last with a man who _said_ he knew all about the port, but) Tj T*
(who turned out to be absolutely worthless. We then made a start, and) Tj T*
(with the exception of meeting with the most violent thunderstorm, in) Tj T*
(which the lightning was something awful, nothing extraordinary occurred) Tj T*
(on our passage across the Gulf of Mexico, and we scarcely saw a) Tj T*
(sail?very different from our experiences between Nassau and Wilmington,) Tj T*
(when it was generally a case of "sail on the port bow" or "steamer right) Tj T*
(ahead" at all hours of the day.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The third evening after leaving Havana we had run our distance, and, on) Tj T*
(heaving the lead and finding that we were within a few miles of the) Tj T*
(shore, we steamed cautiously on in order to try and make out the) Tj T*
(blockading squadron or the land. It was a comparatively calm and very) Tj T*
(dark night, just the one for the purpose, but within an hour all had) Tj T*
(changed and it commenced to blow a regular "Norther," a wind which is) Tj T*
(very prevalent on that coast. Until then I had no idea what a "Norther") Tj T*
(meant; first rain came down in torrents, then out of the inky blackness) Tj T*
(of clouds and rain came furious gusts, until a hurricane was blowing) Tj T*
(against which, notwithstanding that we were steaming at full speed, we) Tj T*
(made little or no way, and although the sea was smooth our decks were) Tj T*
(swept by white foam and spray. Suddenly we made out some dark objects) Tj T*
