() Tj T*
(Before considering the effect of these changes on the future of) Tj T*
(blockading, it will be as well to ascertain what lessons were learnt) Tj T*
(from the blockade of the American coast.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We soon discovered that with due care and pluck the risk was far less) Tj T*
(than people believed; except in a few cases our losses were caused by) Tj T*
(ignorance of position in making the port. In some cases this was owing) Tj T*
(to the fact of our being chased about by day; in others it was caused by) Tj T*
(the irregular action of the Gulf-stream; and in some cases it was due to) Tj T*
(neglect and want of care in keeping a proper look-out at daylight; also) Tj T*
(to not keeping clear of vessels when seen, and to steaming too fast when) Tj T*
(not necessary, thereby causing smoke, which discovered to the blockaders) Tj T*
(the position of the runner. Discovery \(after taking all possible) Tj T*
(precautions\) by a faster vessel was the cause of a small minority of) Tj T*
(captures.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Again, the blockade was carried on on a wrong principle. The Northern) Tj T*
(plan was,?to keep a number of ships close off the port, as a rule) Tj T*
(anchoring by day and by night moving close in, and a few ships at a) Tj T*
(moderate distance from the land. This plan enabled runners to lie out a) Tj T*
(fair distance from the shore at sunset so as to run in when the time) Tj T*
(came, having the whole night before them should they be seen. On coming) Tj T*
(out, we felt that after the first ten miles or so from the shore there) Tj T*
(was little chance of anything seeing us before daylight, and if we were) Tj T*
(seen then the inshore squadron could not join in the chase.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Off Bermuda I rarely saw a cruiser; off the Bahamas there were three or) Tj T*
(four, but not well placed; at sea most of the cruisers were in pairs, as) Tj T*
(far as I could make out; so that their limit of vision was only that of) Tj T*
(one, and in such a case there is always the possibility of the one) Tj T*
(trusting to the other to keep a good look-out.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The action of the Gulf-stream was an important factor in the) Tj T*
(calculations which the blockade-runners had to take into consideration.) Tj T*
(Its rate is so uncertain, that unless you had taken a sight the day) Tj T*
(before you got in you could not depend upon your position, and although) Tj T*
(it could be verified by the soundings it could not be laid down by them) Tj T*
(alone. Star observation, from the uncertain horizon, could not be) Tj T*
(depended upon, and the moon of course was not available; on the other) Tj T*
(hand, the general haze was in our favour.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(That in the future there will ever be a similar blockade is improbable;) Tj T*
(it will be one of armed ships against armed ships, and the only) Tj T*
(exception, if it can be called running a blockade, will be that of armed) Tj T*
(merchant-ships bringing food to England, which will be required to meet) Tj T*
(cruisers on the open sea, and not to run in and out of a blockaded port.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I will now take up the three points of speed, quick-firing guns, and) Tj T*
(search-lights.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To begin with search-lights: on first thoughts the search-light would) Tj T*
