BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
(appear to be a formidable weapon in the hands of the blockader; but on) Tj T*
(consideration I don't think it is so, excepting perhaps in the case of a) Tj T*
(runner being chased at night, or into the night, by a cruiser of equal) Tj T*
(or superior speed which could, by means of her search-light, keep her) Tj T*
(quarry under observation, and, if within range, perhaps speedily sink) Tj T*
(her. In the dash through an inside squadron lying off a port this would) Tj T*
(not apply. True, it would be very uncomfortable for the blockade-runner) Tj T*
(to find herself within the sphere of a dozen search-lights all around) Tj T*
(her, but it would be equally uncomfortable for the ships exhibiting) Tj T*
(those lights were they within range of the protecting fort, as they) Tj T*
(would most probably immediately be plugged by its guns. Moreover, a fort) Tj T*
(supplied with search-lights could be constantly flashing them over the) Tj T*
(area comprised within the range of its guns, and this would tend to) Tj T*
(force a blockading fleet to keep at a more respectable distance and so) Tj T*
(widen out and render the passage between its lines more easy for the) Tj T*
(blockade-runner.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The introduction of the search-light therefore appears to me to be in) Tj T*
(favour of the runner. I assume that the light is in use at the port from) Tj T*
(which the runner starts and is protected by guns. As most likely it will) Tj T*
(be at fixed points, and as there can be no object for secrecy in its) Tj T*
(use, it can be flashed from time to time irregularly so as to show) Tj T*
(whether the vicinity of the port is clear of hostile cruisers or not. No) Tj T*
(cruiser will care to come within range of the light; consequently the) Tj T*
(runner will have the advantage of seeing his road is clear before him) Tj T*
(when he starts, and the further out the cruisers are, the further apart,) Tj T*
(given equal numbers, must they be.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(On the other hand, the blockader wishes to keep his position dark and) Tj T*
(will not use his light for fear of being seen; so it is useless to him.) Tj T*
(Again, a light on the Mound at Fort Fisher would have been invaluable to) Tj T*
(us; the light thrown up into the air would have been of no use to the) Tj T*
(blockader, while to us it would have fixed the position and enabled us) Tj T*
(to run in with confidence. For my part, if in command of a blockader,) Tj T*
(unless it was to call friends to my assistance, I would prefer not to) Tj T*
(use the light.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The present condition of affairs with regard to quick-firing guns and) Tj T*
(the armament of modern war-vessels, in my opinion, would be distinctly) Tj T*
(in favour of the blockader. Seeing how many more of this description of) Tj T*
(gun are carried by our modern ships compared with the slow-firing) Tj T*
(old-fashioned guns of thirty years ago, to say nothing of their) Tj T*
(increased range and accuracy, I fear a blockade-runner would stand a) Tj T*
(poor chance if she allowed herself to come within the range of the guns) Tj T*
(of a cruiser so armed, at all events in daylight. Of course at night,) Tj T*
(and if she were within the range of the guns of a protecting fort, her) Tj T*
(chances would be more equally balanced; as the fort would be supplied) Tj T*
(with similar guns to those of her assailants, and would doubtless use) Tj T*
(them with effect. I am of the opinion, therefore, that the modern gun is) Tj T*
(distinctly in favour of the blockader as compared with the runner. The) Tj T*
(report of the quick-firing gun is much sharper and the flash much more) Tj T*
(brilliant than that of the old-fashioned gun; and this constitutes an) Tj T*
