(additional element in favour of the blockader, for the report and flash,) Tj T*
(being heard and seen at a greater distance, would call any neighbouring) Tj T*
(cruiser to the blockader's assistance.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Though the increase of speed attained by modern ships affects both) Tj T*
(sides, the enormous speed now developed by cruisers and torpedo) Tj T*
(destroyers would seem at first sight to give the blockading force a) Tj T*
(distinct advantage. But if war-vessels have improved their speed) Tj T*
(merchant-steamers have done the same; and, as I have pointed out in) Tj T*
(previous chapters, the blockade-runner has several points in her favour) Tj T*
(by always being in good going condition and on the alert, whereas the) Tj T*
(blockader cannot always have steam handy or be ready for the advent of) Tj T*
(the runner on the scene. If, however, the maritime Power in question) Tj T*
(could afford a large number of exceedingly fast cruisers and torpedo) Tj T*
(catchers to be constantly patrolling the seas adjacent to the blockaded) Tj T*
(ports, and could keep those vessels supplied with coals, I think the) Tj T*
(runner's chances of success would be materially reduced under the new) Tj T*
(regime. But could this be done, seeing the difficulty there would be of) Tj T*
(procuring coal and supplies from perhaps a distant base? There is one) Tj T*
(factor resulting from increased speed which certainly is in favour of) Tj T*
(the runner; that is, in consequence of her being at sea a shorter time) Tj T*
(while making her hazardous passage, her risk is diminished. And this is) Tj T*
(a material point. In the olden days it was considered a fast passage if) Tj T*
(the distance between Wilmington and Nassau, which now could be traversed) Tj T*
(in some thirty hours, was covered in fifty. On the whole, therefore,) Tj T*
(increased speed is in favour of the runner. Speed requires coal, and a) Tj T*
(man who knows what he has to do can economise coal to an extent) Tj T*
(unattainable by the man whose movements are uncertain. He can be either) Tj T*
(going full speed with clear fires, or be ready for it to a greater) Tj T*
(extent than a man who is waiting until his speed is required. As) Tj T*
(probably in the future there will not be short runs from shallow ports,) Tj T*
(the runner can be of a size equal to, if not greater than, the) Tj T*
(blockader; consequently, unless in smooth water, more likely to attain) Tj T*
(greater speed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A point of great importance, which should not be overlooked, is the) Tj T*
(effect of national character. In the American war, with the exception of) Tj T*
(one or two Danes, all the officers and crews of the runners were either) Tj T*
(British or Southerners. It is a question whether any other European) Tj T*
(State would show sufficient spirit of enterprise to carry a blockade on) Tj T*
(a large scale to a successful issue. What is wanted in blockade-runners) Tj T*
(is not only capable leaders, but a large number of people who will trust) Tj T*
(each other and their leaders.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Hitherto I have only considered the question of evading a superior force) Tj T*
(outside, and of being prepared to run and not to fight unless necessary.) Tj T*
